sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:15:1. **Aggregate prediction works; individual twins don't.** GPT-4-simulated populations predict treatment effects of survey experiments at r = 0.85 (r = 0.90 on unpublished, contamination-proof studies) [Hewitt et al. 2024]. But individual-level digital twins correlate with their humans at only r ≈ 0.20 [Funhouse Mirrors mega-study, 2025, 19 pre-registered sub-studies]. **Sell population-level effect prediction, never "digital clones."** The phrase "digital clone of users" should be banned from the pitch deck — buyers who know this literature will read it as overclaiming.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:16:2. **The persona *clustering* is not where prediction accuracy comes from.** Demographic/archetype persona prompts explain <10% of human response variance [Hu & Collier, ACL 2024] and barely beat a no-persona base LLM [Funhouse Mirrors]. What does move accuracy: **individual-level conditioning** (interviews/behavioral rows ≫ archetypes ≫ demographics: 86% vs 74% normalized accuracy in Park et al. 2024), **calibration against historical experiments** (fine-tuning on past A/B tests: 65% → 82–84% pairwise winner accuracy in LOLA, Marketing Science 2025), and **distribution elicitation methodology** (fixes mode collapse).
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:17:3. **Therefore the highest-leverage controllable component is the *conditioning + calibration + evaluation stack*, of which the persona pack is the scaffolding — not the engine.** Personas remain essential as the stratification, interpretation, and product-communication layer. But the conditioning signal each agent receives must become individual-level, and the system's accuracy claims must rest on a backtest harness, not on cluster quality metrics.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:18:4. **The current `sidira-personas` pipeline is a competent scaffold with real defects** (detailed in §2): hard assignment discards the GMM's own posteriors, the stability metric is non-standard and likely flattering, the planned supervised classifier is mostly unnecessary, zero test coverage, and the JTBD/motivation fields personas assert are behavioral proxies the data can't certify — which the LLM will then faithfully simulate as if true.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:19:5. **For the Meta/Google/Amazon sale, the benchmark IS the product.** Ron Kohavi (built ExP at Microsoft, ran experimentation at Airbnb) has publicly attacked this exact product category (Nov 2025): synthetic users "hide untestable assumptions inside the LLM." These buyers know that ~67–90% of their experiments don't win, that humans predict outcomes below chance (Kohavi's survey: 2.3/8 average), and that "always predict flat" gets 90% raw accuracy. The only thing that moves them is a blind, pre-committed, properly-nulled prediction track record scored against the right baselines (§4).
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:28:| 4 | **Soft membership end-to-end**: GMM posteriors carried into sampling, assignment, and monitoring; kill the planned XGBoost classifier except for cold-start | Free (already computed), fixes variance collapse + the label-noise classifier problem |
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:29:| 5 | **Distribution elicitation + calibration layer in the simulator**: predict deltas vs control, unblind the design, elevated temperature / verbalized sampling / SSR-style rating elicitation, learned effect-size shrinkage | LLM effect sizes are direction-right magnitude-inflated [Hewitt]; temperature=0 single-sample is the documented mode-collapse recipe |
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:38:accuracy = f( conditioning signal richness     ← individual data ≫ archetypes ≫ demographics
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:40:            , calibration to historical data   ← fine-tune/shrink on past experiments: biggest single lever
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:41:            , treatment modality               ← text/copy r≈0.85 ··· UI/behavioral r≈0.25 (hard ceiling)
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:43:            , hypothesis description quality   ← confirmed: unblinding the design fixes causal confounding [Gui & Toubia]
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:49:- **Hewitt et al. 2024**: 70 pre-registered survey experiments, 476 treatment effects. GPT-4 simulated respondents: **r = 0.85** predicted-vs-actual effects; **r = 0.90 on unpublished studies**; beats human forecasters. BUT on 250-treatment megastudies (tiny, noisy effects — the regime closest to mature product A/B): **r = 0.34**. Effect magnitudes systematically inflated; require regression calibration.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:52:- **LOLA 2025** (Upworthy, 17.7k headline tests): prompting picks the better headline at ~65% pairwise (near chance on multi-arm winner: 46.3%); **fine-tuned models: 82–84% pairwise**. The winning architecture was **simulation-as-prior inside Thompson sampling**, not simulation-as-replacement.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:54:- **Chen et al. 2025**: 276 published field experiments, 78% directional — but bimodal, collapsing on ethnicity/norms/ethics treatments, contamination not ruled out.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:58:- "Crawling/feature pack is deterministic" — agreed, and Gui & Toubia adds: the *prompt must unblind the experimental design* (state what is randomized, what is held fixed) or the simulation confounds treatment with imagined covariates.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:60:- "Personas are the most important thing we control" — **revised**: the conditioning stack (which includes personas), the calibration loop, and the evaluation harness are what you control. Persona *cluster quality* (ARI 0.67 vs 0.75) is weakly coupled to prediction accuracy; persona *conditioning depth* (archetype vs individual) is strongly coupled.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:69:- **Weights as a first-class output** (head_count / order / revenue), Hamilton largest-remainder stratified sampling, deterministic identity seeds, paired treatment/control sampling sharing seeds — this is *better experimental hygiene than most vendors in this space have*. The paired-sampling design is exactly right for delta prediction.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:79:1. **Hard assignment discards GMM posteriors** (`cluster.py: assign()` exports hard labels + max-prob only). With overlapping components (ARI 0.67 ⇒ boundaries are mush), ~30–40% of probability mass is misassigned *by construction*. Every simulated agent then inherits a falsely crisp identity. Fix is free: the model already computes posteriors.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:82:4. **Tandem-analysis bias + model mismatch.** PCA-then-GMM is the classic criticized "tandem" design (PCA can rotate cluster structure into discarded components); fixed 8-dim per group is an arbitrary block-weighting; one-hot categoricals z-scored into PCA + Gaussian components is a likelihood mismatch (BIC then adds components to fit non-Gaussianity, not real subpopulations). k_range [4,8] is arbitrary; no ICL beside BIC.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:83:5. **No holdout anything**: all 8,823 users used for fit + profiling; no train/test; **zero unit tests in the repo** (`tests/__init__.py` is empty); planned supervised assignment classifier not implemented (good — see §3.5, mostly shouldn't be).
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:90:3. **Absolute-level prediction**: A/B mode already computes treatment−control deltas on paired seeds (good), but single-arm mode predicts levels, and no shrinkage/calibration layer exists anywhere. LLM effect magnitudes are systematically inflated [Hewitt]; ship a learned shrinkage layer from day one of the backtest era.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:91:4. **No no-persona control arm.** Much of any persona panel's apparent signal is base-model behavior [Funhouse Mirrors]. Every accuracy claim must be reported as *delta over a no-persona base-LLM run* — also a cheap, differentiating honesty artifact.
