sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:130:### 3.3 Elicitation & calibration in the simulator
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:133:2. **Unblind the design** in the prompt: explicitly state what changed and what is held fixed (price, quality, everything else equal) [Gui & Toubia].
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:135:4. **Learned shrinkage layer**: regression from (predicted effect, realized effect) pairs accumulated in the backtest harness; report calibrated effects with intervals. Until data exists, apply a fixed conservative shrinkage prior anchored on Hewitt's documented inflation.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:144:- **Inject measured response priors into persona cards**: "this segment showed +9% lift to free-shipping promos, null response to UI redesigns." The agent now reasons from causal facts, not vibes.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:147:### 3.5 New-user assignment ("the classifier")
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:171:- **Their self-knowledge**: Kohavi's surveys show their own people predict outcomes below chance (avg 2.3/8 on 3-class). The opportunity: the bar to beat *human triage* is low and they know it quantitatively.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:172:- **The direct attack**: Kohavi, Nov 2025, on synthetic-user A/B simulation: "you can't bypass the fundamental problem of causal inference; you're just hiding untestable assumptions inside the LLM." This post will be quoted in every procurement review. The only viable positioning: **"we beat your current prioritization prior and warm-start your experiments — we do not replace them."** LOLA (peer-reviewed, Marketing Science) is the citation that this architecture (simulation-as-prior + real traffic correction) beats both pure A/B and pure LLM.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:174:- **The modality cliff**: published evidence: text/copy treatments r ≈ 0.85; behavioral/field/non-text r ≈ 0.25–0.34. Most of a Meta/Amazon roadmap (ranking, latency, pricing, layout) is in the weak regime. **Publish the stratified honesty table** (accuracy by treatment modality, including "we are near-chance here" cells). A vendor claiming uniform 90% accuracy is read — correctly — as a fraud signal by this audience; published failure regimes are read as competence.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:181:| 3-class directional (win/flat/lose) **balanced** accuracy / MCC | always-flat null; base-rate random | never raw accuracy |
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:182:| Brier + ECE on P(ship-positive) | climatology (predict base rate always) | report resolution vs calibration decomposition |
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:190:**Power discipline**: distinguishing 60% from 50% directional accuracy at 80% power needs ~150–200 experiments. Never claim from a 20-experiment pilot; sophisticated buyers will size this themselves and dismiss small-n claims.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:195:1. **Upworthy confirmatory-set blind eval** — 22,743 held-out headline tests (exploratory 4,873 allowed for tuning). Persona-conditioned simulation vs: random, GPT-prompting (~65% pairwise / 46.3% multi-arm), LOLA's fine-tuned baselines (82–84% pairwise). Target: beat prompting decisively, approach fine-tuned, with calibrated margins. Honest caveat: text/CTR is the easy regime — say so.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:196:2. **Hewitt archive + megastudies eval** (476 + 346 treatment effects, includes unpublished = contamination-controlled; Milkman gym/vaccine megastudies for the hard tiny-effect regime). Target: r ≥ 0.6 on field/behavioral effects with correct top-5 rank ordering beats both published LLM and human-forecaster anchors.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:199:5. **End-to-end pipeline eval on Hillstrom + Criteo Uplift** — the only public datasets with individual user features AND measured treatment responses, hence the only public test of the full persona-construction → simulation stack (§6.1). Items 1–4 validate the simulation engine with synthetic demographic panels; item 5 validates the pipeline.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:201:**Tier 1 — first design partner (the artifact that closes deals):**
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:202:6. **Buyer-archive blind backtest**: 150–200 of *their* historical experiments; Sidira receives specs/screenshots only; predictions (3-class + effect-size distribution) **hash-committed before unblinding**; contamination screen for publicly-leaked tests; scored per §4.2 against always-flat, their archive's empirical-Bayes prior, a metadata GBM, and averaged internal PM forecasts. Win condition: statistically significant lift over the best non-simulation baseline at n ≥ 150 + demonstrated non-filtering of top-decile winners. This doubles as the Layer-F calibration data.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:205:7. **Prospective escrow registry**: predictions filed before each experiment starts, third-party timestamped (Social Science Prediction Platform is the template), scored and published quarterly. The only design that fully answers contamination; no vendor in this space has one — first mover gets the category's only audited track record.
