sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:260:**The structural consequence:** Upworthy/Hewitt/Milkman have no user-level behavioral data, so Tier-0 artifacts 1–4 validate the **simulation engine** (elicitation, calibration, delta prediction, null integrity) under demographic-grade conditioning — a deliberate **lower bound**: "even with generic panels we score X; behavioral conditioning adds Y per Park et al." They cannot validate the persona-extraction pipeline. **Hillstrom and Criteo are the only public end-to-end tests**: cluster real user features → build personas → simulate the treatment → score against measured uplift, including the GATES-style check that our personas stratify the real treatment effect. Narrow regimes (email/ads, conversion outcomes) and Criteo's features are anonymized — but it is the one public proof the full stack can exist before a design partner does. Hence Tier-0 artifact #5. Full-stack validation on product-grade experiments remains a Tier-1 (customer-archive) deliverable; say so plainly when presenting Tier-0 numbers.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:264:This analysis covers how the change description should be *used* — design unblinding (§3.3), paraphrase ensembles (§3.3), hypothesis linting (§1), OEC-aligned scoring (§4.4 Q11) — but did **not** audit how the crawler's feature pack and the user's change PRD are currently ingested, diffed, and rendered into agent context. That scoping followed the founder premise ("crawling is deterministic; hypothesis quality is user-dependent"), and the premise hid a real component: **treatment rendering** — (existing state, proposed change) → the exact text/image context an agent reads — is its own design surface with documented accuracy consequences (prompt-format sensitivity [Sclar]; the modality cliff §4.1). Two requirements fall out:
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:273:**LLM simulation accuracy:** Hewitt, Ashokkumar, Ghezae & Willer 2024 (70 experiments, r=0.85/0.90; treatmenteffect.app) · Park et al. 2024, arXiv:2411.10109 (1,052 interview agents, 85% of test-retest; interviews ≫ demographics) · Toubia et al. 2025, Twin-2K-500, arXiv:2505.17479 (public 2,058-person testbed) · Peng et al. 2025/26 "Funhouse Mirrors," arXiv:2509.19088 (individual r≈0.20; five distortions) · SimBench, arXiv:2510.17516 (40.8/100; alignment–simulation tradeoff) · Chen, Hu & Lu 2025, arXiv:2504.01167 (78% on 276 field experiments, bimodal) · Aher et al. ICML 2023 (Turing Experiments; hyper-accuracy) · Argyle et al. 2023, Political Analysis (silicon sampling) · Horton 2023, NBER w31122.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:275:**Elicitation/calibration:** Meister, Guestrin & Hashimoto 2024, arXiv:2411.05403 (distributional alignment) · Zhang et al., Verbalized Sampling, arXiv:2510.01171 · Maier et al. (PyMC Labs×Colgate), arXiv:2510.08338 (SSR, 90% of test-retest) · arXiv:2510.11408 (rectification with small human samples) · arXiv:2502.04262 (PPI for experiments) · arXiv:2502.17773 (effective sample size of an LLM).
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:276:**A/B prediction & experimentation culture:** Ye et al., LOLA, Marketing Science 2025, arXiv:2406.02611 (Upworthy; 65%→82–84%; simulation-as-prior) · Kohavi, Tang & Xu 2020, Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments · Kohavi & Thomke HBR 2017 · Kohavi LinkedIn Nov 2025 (synthetic-user critique) · Azevedo et al., JPE 2020 (fat tails: 2% of ideas = 75% of gains) · Goldberg & Johndrow 2017 (eBay empirical-Bayes priors) · Coey & Cunningham WWW 2019 (experiment splitting, attenuation) · Athey et al. surrogate index, NBER w26463 · Netflix 200-test surrogate eval, arXiv:2311.11922 · DellaVigna & Pope, JPE 2018 + Social Science Prediction Platform · Upworthy Research Archive, Nature Sci Data 2021 (32,487 tests) · Liu & Cardoso, NeurIPS 2021 D&B (OCE dataset scarcity) · Milkman et al., Nature 2021 (megastudies) · Meta WW/WES arXiv:2004.05363 · Google RecSim NG arXiv:2103.08057 · AgentA/B arXiv:2504.09723 (Amazon-affiliated; closest prior art).
sessions/persona-classifier-research/analysis.md:277:**Segmentation methodology:** Wedel & Kamakura 2000, Market Segmentation · Hennig 2007 CSDA (clusterboot; Jaccard thresholds) · Biernacki et al. 2000 (ICL) · Tibshirani & Walther 2005 (prediction strength) · von Luxburg 2010 (stability caveats) · Şenbabaoğlu et al. 2014 (consensus-clustering null) · Escofier & Pagès 1994 (MFA) · McLachlan et al. 2003 (mixtures of factor analyzers) · Baek et al. 2010 (MCFA) · Cutler & Breiman 1994 (archetypal analysis) · Chavent et al. (PCAmix/FAMD) · Kamakura & Wedel JMR 1997 + Gilula et al. JMR 2006 (data fusion) · Wager & Athey JASA 2018 + Athey et al. 2019 (causal forests/grf) · Künzel et al. PNAS 2019 (X-learner) · Chernozhukov et al. (GATES) · Northcutt et al. JAIR 2021 (label noise) · Niculescu-Mizil & Caruana ICML 2005 (calibration) · Grinsztajn et al. NeurIPS 2022 (tabular DL vs trees).
sessions/persona-classifier-research/qa-founder-clarifications.md:5:## 1. Product intent: directional, never statistically significant
sessions/persona-classifier-research/qa-founder-clarifications.md:7:Founder clarification: Sidira is never intended to produce statistically significant results — only promising, directionally correct reads. **This matches the evidence exactly** (aggregate direction works, r=0.85; individual clones don't, r≈0.20). One trap to avoid: at big tech, only 8–33% of experiments win, so "always predict flat" is directionally correct 67–92% of the time while being useless. The directional claim must therefore be framed as *"we re-rank your backlog better than your current heuristics"* — measured against the always-flat baseline, not against zero. The bar is low (their own people predict below chance) but must be beaten demonstrably.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/qa-founder-clarifications.md:19:"Eventually" never arrives: backlogs grow faster than test capacity drains them, so the back of a re-ranked queue gets overtaken, goes stale, or its surface gets redesigned — de-facto elimination without Sidira owning the kill decision. The savings channels:
sessions/persona-classifier-research/qa-founder-clarifications.md:27:- *Why valuable*: A/B tests say WHAT, never WHY; chat fills the explanation gap their platforms don't cover. Unique capability hiding in our paired-seed design: **the counterfactual interview** — the same agent identity experienced both control and treatment, so you can ask it what changed. No real-world UXR can do that. Make this the centerpiece.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/qa-founder-clarifications.md:34:**No.** What's retired: the persona archetype as the *only* conditioning signal, and ARI-chasing as the goal. What personas still do (all load-bearing): (1) sampling backbone — the GMM is the compression layer that answers Q2; (2) half of every agent's prompt — the gestalt that makes an individual row coherent; (3) the unit of insight PMs can act on ("alienates high-frequency DashPass users, 44% of volume"); (4) the container where measured response priors accumulate (the long-term moat); (5) the legible cast for the chat feature. One line: **personas are the scaffolding, not the engine** — accuracy comes from individual conditioning + calibration + elicitation; personas make the system organized, explainable, and sellable.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/qa-founder-clarifications.md:40:Has: the full behavioral architecture (hierarchical conditioning, soft sampling, distribution elicitation, delta prediction, shrinkage) and the feature framework — decision rule: *a feature earns its place if it improves held-out experiment prediction, not cluster stability*; three distinct feature roles (clustering basis ≠ conditioning signal ≠ response priors); measured-vs-proxy flagging; survey fusion.
sessions/persona-classifier-research/qa-founder-clarifications.md:45:Q: public benchmarks need (existing state, proposed change, personas) — where do they come from? A: proposed changes are in the archives (headline texts, stimuli, message texts); existing state is reconstructable (Wayback Machine for Upworthy; survey/program frames elsewhere); **personas are the gap** — no public archive has user-level behavioral data, so Tier-0 artifacts 1–4 run on demographic-calibrated synthetic panels and validate the *simulation engine* only (an honest lower bound). **Hillstrom (64K customers, real RFM features) and Criteo Uplift (13M users) are the only public end-to-end tests of the full persona pipeline** → added as Tier-0 artifact #5. Q: did the analysis audit how the current-feature PRD / change PRD are ingested? A: it covers how the description should be used (unblinding, paraphrase ensembles, linting, OEC scoring) but did NOT audit the ingestion/rendering path — the **treatment-rendering layer** (state + change → what the agent reads) is a newly named component; it must be format-identical between benchmark and production (build it once, in the harness, first) and is now in scope for the agent behavior spec.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/context.md:17:  - **#221 (B) — ~done, precise audit:** SHIPPED = distribution elicitation (#232, `reaction_elicitor.py` verbalized sampling), bootstrap CI on distributions (#234), no-persona control + persona-lift (#237), delta-mode default (#239), per-metric elicitation + blinded/unblinded renderer (#225/#226/#229). **REMAINING = (i) paraphrase ensemble K=3** (specced in `elicitation-templates.md`, NOT built — buildable now, small, opt-in) + **(ii) learned shrinkage-layer interface** (Hewitt-inflation; the `scoring.py` "shrinkage" is unrelated Bayesian-toward-0.5 sentiment shrinkage — the *fit* is #222-gated, only the interface is buildable now) + sentiment-score re-fit (#222-gated). 
