sessions/agent-behavior-spec/context.md:54:3. **Elicitation prompt templates** (tie to #221): design unblinding, distribution/verbalized sampling, delta-vs-control framing, paraphrase ensemble, no-persona control prompt.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/context.md:55:   - **3a. Per-metric outcome elicitation (NEW, 2026-06-13 discussion).** Today the agent is only asked to "react" → classified to 3-way sentiment; the metric the PM actually cares about ("conversion +5%") is never elicited, and even A/B `lift` is in sentiment-score units. This spec must define, **per metric type**, the exact behavioral decision the agent is asked (conversion → "would you complete checkout?"; retention → "would you come back next week?"; CTR → "would you click this?"), elicited as a distribution not a point. The aggregation (rate per arm → paired-bootstrap lift in native units) lives in #221; the *question design* lives here. Decision rule: the question must encode the metric's actual user action, framed in-character, never "rate your sentiment." Magnitude is direction-credible but inflated → output stays directional/relative until #222's shrinkage layer calibrates absolute lift (Upworthy = first CTR calibration target).
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/context.md:64:- `backend/tests/test_behavioral_profile.py` — §2 renderer tests (14): salience threshold (inclusive boundary), direction/magnitude buckets, deterministic ordering, no-centroid/all-typical/all-malformed honesty paths, non-finite skip, humanize suffix-strip, tunable threshold, block shape
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/context.md:70:- `sessions/agent-behavior-spec/ablation-protocol.md` — §4 deliverable: which upgrades earn their place — value-ladder + knockout design, outcome metrics vs baselines/nulls, pre-registration, build-order decisions
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/context.md:74:- `sessions/agent-behavior-spec/elicitation-conversion.md` — §3a deliverable: the conversion question (prompt text, output schema, aggregation contract, design rationale, open Qs)
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/context.md:83:- Positioning guardrail carries through: never "digital clones"; chat is hypothesis generator, never evidence.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/context.md:84:- **Metric-prediction gap (2026-06-13, code-confirmed):** current output is generic — `confidence_score = 0.6·sentiment + 0.4·(1−weakness_share)`; A/B `lift` is in confidence-score units (sentiment delta), not the hypothesis's metric. `prompt_composer.py` asks "react to the change"; `scoring.py` classifies sentiment + weakness. Predicting a named metric (conversion lift) needs: (1) target-metric field on `Hypothesis` [#221], (2) per-metric behavioral elicitation [this spec §3a], (3) rate-per-arm aggregation reusing the #90 paired bootstrap [#221]. Directional/relative lift is near-term; trustworthy absolute % is gated on #222's calibration.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/oec-scoring.md:14:weakness_signal  = 1 − any_flag_share
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/oec-scoring.md:15:confidence_score = (0.6·sentiment_score + 0.4·weakness_signal) × 100
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/oec-scoring.md:19:**The gap, stated plainly: this is OEC-blind.** A customer optimizing **conversion**, one optimizing **7-day retention**, and one optimizing **revenue per session** all receive the *same* `confidence_score` — a generic "how warmly did synthetic users receive this." Kohavi's whole point is that an experiment's success is defined by *its OEC*; scoring against generic reception is scoring against the wrong target. The `lift` being in "confidence_score units" is the same gap in the A/B path (already captured in #221).
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/oec-scoring.md:28:- **Headline = the primary-OEC lift** from §3a's per-metric elicitation + §3d delta aggregation — e.g. "+4.2pp simulated conversion lift [CI], directional/rank pre-calibration." This is the number the customer optimizes, in the customer's units.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/oec-scoring.md:30:- **Weakness → stays as the experiment-design critique** (it's already OEC-adjacent: `wrong_metric`, `wrong_cohort`, `underpowered_design` are critiques of the *test*, not the reception). Keep it as the design-quality sidebar.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/oec-scoring.md:32:So the three current signals don't disappear — they're **re-ranked**: OEC lift becomes the headline, sentiment becomes diagnostic, weakness stays as design critique.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/oec-scoring.md:38:A change can move the primary OEC the right way while tripping a guardrail — conversion up *because* a dark-pattern also spiked refund requests. Kohavi-grade scoring must catch this:
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/oec-scoring.md:54:- When an OEC is set, `SimulationResult`'s headline shifts from `confidence_score` to the **primary-OEC lift** (native units); `confidence_score` is retained as a secondary "reception" field, not removed (backwards-compatible, and genuinely informative as a divergence signal).
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/oec-scoring.md:56:- **Calibration gating carries through (§3a §5, §4):** OEC lift is reported **directional/rank/relative** until #222's shrinkage layer calibrates absolute magnitude (Upworthy → CTR first). The headline pre-calibration reads "treatment beats control on conversion, CI excludes zero, ranks #2 of 6" — never a bare "+4.2pp" as truth.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/oec-scoring.md:62:- **`Hypothesis`** (#221): target-metric field carries `primary_oec` + `guardrails[]` (each = metric name + elicitation template selector + direction).
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/oec-scoring.md:82:    — and #222 calibration converts directional lift → trustworthy magnitude
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/oec-scoring.md:85:Honesty guardrails run end to end: blinded elicitation (no demand characteristics), delta cancels uncalibrated propensity, modality cliff sets the confidence ceiling, directional-until-calibrated, every fallback logged, chat is hypothesis-not-evidence. That through-line *is* the Kohavi-proof positioning the analysis demanded: "calibrated prior / triage / warm-start," never "digital clones" or an uncalibrated magnitude.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/oec-scoring.md:91:1. **Composite OEC weighting:** when a customer has a multi-metric OEC (Kohavi often a single combined metric), do we score each component separately and let the PM weigh, or elicit a combined preference? (recommend: separate components + explicit guardrails for v1 — a single blended OEC number hides the tradeoff that's the whole insight.)
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/treatment-rendering-layer.md:29:   - **The agent sees TODAY in full structure and the PROPOSAL as prose.** It compares a wireframe against a paragraph. Any change whose effect lives in structure (a moved button, a new gate, a removed step, a reordered flow) is invisible unless the prose happens to spell it out — exactly the UI-modality changes the research says are already the hardest regime (r≈0.25–0.34).
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/treatment-rendering-layer.md:37:## 1. Design principle
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/treatment-rendering-layer.md:80:| **Copy / text** | `component.text` deltas | inline `"old" → "new"` | strong (r≈0.85) — trust most |
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/treatment-rendering-layer.md:81:| **UI structure** | screen/component/transition adds-removes-moves | the structural changelog above | weak (r≈0.25–0.34) — flag on the result |
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/treatment-rendering-layer.md:94:- **Benchmark (#222):** an archived experiment (e.g. Upworthy headline A vs B) → adapter that builds the *same* `Hypothesis` shape (control headline = current `text`, treatment headline = proposed `text`, everything else "NOT changing") → renderer.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/treatment-rendering-layer.md:96:So the adapter work lives at the *edge* (archive → `Hypothesis`), and the renderer is shared. If a benchmark can't be expressed as a `Hypothesis` + state-diff, that's a signal the production path can't represent that change class either — a useful forcing function, not a workaround to route around.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/chat-and-counterfactual.md:4:**Scope:** how an agent stays the *same person* across a multi-turn chat, and how the paired-seed design enables the **counterfactual interview** — the unique mechanic where one identity's reaction under control and under treatment can be put side by side. qa §5's verdict frames the whole section: **strongest product feature and weakest scientific claim simultaneously.** The job here is to make it powerful *and* keep it from lying.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/chat-and-counterfactual.md:35:## 2. The counterfactual interview — the honest design
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/chat-and-counterfactual.md:39:**The defensible design: juxtaposition, not introspection.**
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/chat-and-counterfactual.md:61:- **Tie clusters to the weakness taxonomy** (`Weakness.category`: wrong_metric / wrong_cohort / broken_mechanism / untestable_hypothesis / hidden_guardrail_risk / underpowered_design) so the interview output is structured, comparable across runs, and actionable — not a wall of free text.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/chat-and-counterfactual.md:69:- **Predicted complaint themes vs realized post-launch complaints.** After a change actually ships, mine real signal (app-store reviews, support tickets, UXR notes) for complaint themes; score the panel's *predicted* theme distribution against the realized one (rank/overlap of themes, same directional bar as §4 — not exact frequencies).
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/chat-and-counterfactual.md:70:- This is a distinct outcome from the metric backtest (§4 ablation): that validates *what* (direction/rank of lift); this validates *why* (theme prediction). Two differentiators, two evidence tracks.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/chat-and-counterfactual.md:71:- Slots into the evidence ladder as a Tier-1+ artifact (needs a shipped change + post-launch signal → customer or public-launch data); pre-register the theme taxonomy before scoring (§4 §5 discipline).
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/context-assembly.md:23:1. **No individual behavioral row → no within-persona quantitative variety.** Of the four identity blocks, two are persona-level constants and the third (`IdentitySeed`) is thin demographic texture. Two agents in the same persona differ only by a few demographic bands + two free-text lines + LLM temperature. The research's core finding is that accuracy comes from *individual* conditioning — each agent carrying one real user's behavioral texture (the fee-transparency worked example, qa §6: same persona, promo-code Android user vs DashPass holder → opposite grounded reactions). That signal does not exist in the current assembly.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/context-assembly.md:42:Layers 1–3 and 5 exist (1–2 via §1, 3 today, 5 via §3a). **Layer 4 is what §2 adds.** The design principle: the gestalt (layer 3) says what's *typical* for this persona; the row (layer 4) says where *this individual diverges* from typical. The two must not restate each other — the row is rendered as **deltas**, not as a full feature dump.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/context-assembly.md:52:| Production / Tier-0 artifact #5 (Hillstrom, Criteo) | one **real anonymized** user row from the persona's GMM component | the full pipeline end-to-end (real user features → response) |
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/context-assembly.md:62:- Render **direction and rough magnitude**, not raw numbers ("much more often," "rarely," "about average except…") — agents read calibrated comparatives better than decimals, same lesson as §3a's 10-trial framing.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/context-assembly.md:79:## 4. Soft membership (ties to #7, killed-hard-assignment finding)
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/context-assembly.md:81:The analysis killed hard GMM assignment as the *conditioning* mechanism. For assembly:
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/context-assembly.md:83:- **Gestalt (layer 3): hard-assign for v1** — one persona = one cast member, which keeps chat legible and the panel composition interpretable (personas-as-scaffolding, qa §6). A soft-blended narrative is hard to read and harder to chat with.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/context-assembly.md:84:- **Row (layer 4): carries its soft posterior.** The row is sampled from the component it actually belongs to; if an agent sits between two components (posterior 0.7/0.3), that's a real signal — note it as a hedge ("you mostly behave like {A}, with a streak of {B}"). This is where the soft-posterior value lands without sacrificing legibility.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/context-assembly.md:92:- **`compose_identity_prompt` is the frozen-identity entry point (R2-persisted, never recomputed) → high blast radius. Run `impact({target: "compose_identity_prompt", direction: "upstream"})` before any edit** and report the radius, per the repo rule. §1 and §2 both land here — sequence them as one change to that function, not two.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/context-assembly.md:100:1. **Salience threshold |z| ≳ 0.5** — tune on the harness (#222); too low = prompt bloat + diluted signal, too high = agents collapse back toward the gestalt. (recommend: 0.5 start, treat as a tunable, measure on Tier-0 artifact #5.)
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/context-assembly.md:101:2. **Real-row privacy in production** — anonymized real rows carry re-identification risk at the tail (whales, rare segments). GMM-sampled synthetic rows are privacy-clean but one step removed from reality. (recommend: synthetic-by-default in production; real rows only for the Hillstrom/Criteo benchmark where the data is already public/consented.)
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/elicitation-templates.md:10:## 3b. Design unblinding (the biggest single bias lever)
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/elicitation-templates.md:42:- The agent's inflated absolute propensity is a per-agent constant that **cancels in the delta** → the panel-mean delta is direction- and rank-credible *before* #222 calibration; the absolute level is not.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/elicitation-templates.md:43:- **Never frame the delta to the agent.** Each agent answers about *one* state in isolation (blinded, §3b); the subtraction happens in aggregation, never in the prompt. Asking an agent "how much *more* would you do X under the change?" re-introduces the demand characteristic 3b removes — it tells the agent a change exists and asks it to rationalize a direction. The delta is an analyst operation, not an agent question.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/elicitation-templates.md:51:LLM outputs are surface-form sensitive — the same question reworded shifts the answer. To stop a single prompt's idiosyncrasy from masquerading as signal:
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/elicitation-templates.md:65:2. **Row only, no persona gestalt** — does the individual row carry the accuracy, with the persona narrative stripped? (the research predicts most of the signal is here.)
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/elicitation-conversion.md:8:## 0. Design constraints (from the research; why the obvious question is wrong)
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/elicitation-conversion.md:17:| Uncalibrated absolute magnitude | Hewitt 2024 | Absolute P(convert) is direction-right but inflated — never trust the level |
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/elicitation-conversion.md:19:Five design responses, each mapped to a constraint:
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/elicitation-conversion.md:25:5. **Score the DELTA on the paired identity, never the level.** The same agent identity (paired seed, #90) faces control *and* treatment; `lift_i = p_conv(treatment_i) − p_conv(control_i)`. The inflated absolute propensity is a per-agent constant that **cancels in the delta** — this is what makes lift usable *before* the #222 calibration layer exists.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/elicitation-conversion.md:51:The user turn of the **blinded metric pass**. `{...}` come from the Hypothesis target-metric field (#221) + the treatment-rendering layer (the on-screen options). The wording is itself blinded — no "the change", no "you just saw", nothing presuming a prior reaction or signalling an intervention is under test.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/elicitation-conversion.md:131:- **Modality cliff:** conversion is a *behavioral* outcome → the weak regime (published r≈0.25–0.34), materially weaker than copy/CTR (r≈0.85). Surface the regime on the card; do not present conversion lift with the same confidence as a headline test.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/elicitation-conversion.md:132:- **Pre-calibration (before #222):** report **rank + direction + relative** lift ("treatment beats control, CI excludes zero, #2 of 6 variants"). Do NOT print a bare "+5.0pp" as truth — the delta cancels per-agent bias but the *scale* is still uncalibrated until the shrinkage layer is fit (Upworthy → CTR first, then conversion on Hillstrom/Criteo).
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/elicitation-conversion.md:138:## 6. Design decisions (resolved for v1 — revisit if data argues otherwise)
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/ablation-protocol.md:29:Full factorial is ~thousands of cells — not run. The design below is two targeted slices that together give sufficiency *and* necessity.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/ablation-protocol.md:37:1. **Direction accuracy** — sign(predicted lift) == sign(realized lift), as a rate. **Scored against the always-flat baseline** (qa §1: flat is right 67–92% of the time, so beating zero is meaningless; beating flat is the bar).
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/ablation-protocol.md:39:3. **Calibration (gated)** — predicted vs realized magnitude *after* the #222 shrinkage layer. Pre-calibration this is reported as "uncalibrated, rank-only"; it's an outcome only once the shrinkage layer exists.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/ablation-protocol.md:41:Datasets (analysis §6): Upworthy (CTR, strong-modality, the first calibration target) · Hillstrom + Criteo (conversion/uplift, the only real end-to-end persona-pipeline test, artifact #5) · demographic-synthetic artifacts 1–4 (engine-only lower bound, no behavioral row → identity-depth knob degrades to generic there, which is itself a measurement).
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/ablation-protocol.md:45:## 3. Design — two slices, not a factorial
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/ablation-protocol.md:47:**Slice A — the value ladder (sufficiency).** Start from the current production config (prose-only · unblinded · point · persona-only · K=1) as rung 0. Add one upgrade at a time, in the §-predicted value order, measuring the *marginal* gain in rank-ρ and direction-accuracy at each rung:
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/ablation-protocol.md:67:## 4. Statistical protocol (Kohavi-proof, analysis §4)
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/ablation-protocol.md:70:- **Null-integrity certificate (mandatory):** scramble the experiment outcome labels → every cell must score at chance on direction and ρ≈0 on rank. A cell that "predicts" scrambled outcomes is leaking; the whole run is void until it doesn't. Ship the certificate with results.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/ablation-protocol.md:71:- **Multiple-comparisons honesty:** the ladder + knockouts + depth-OFAT is many comparisons. Pre-register the primary endpoint (rank-ρ on Upworthy + artifact #5), correct the secondaries, and **report every cell run** — no dropping the ones that didn't help (silent-truncation rule, same as §1/§2).
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/ablation-protocol.md:79:1. Primary endpoint: rank-ρ on Upworthy + Hillstrom/Criteo.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/ablation-protocol.md:91:- The whole §4 result is also **Tier-0 sales evidence** (analysis §4.5): "here is what each modeling choice buys, measured on public experiments, certified against nulls" — the engine-validation half of the Kohavi-proof story, needing no customer.
sessions/agent-behavior-spec/ablation-protocol.md:97:1. **Held-out split:** leave-one-experiment-out (max data, costly) vs a fixed train/test split per dataset? (recommend: LOO on the small sets Upworthy/Hillstrom; fixed split on Criteo's 13M for cost.)
